# Erdős Problem #99 — Target A Schur demand as exterior-sink surplus

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Run type:** exact algebraic reformulation  
**Target:** Sections 99, 102, 174  
**Depends on:** dual Hall/lattice-deficit identity; plateau-terminalized Schur normal form; tube-shield sector balance  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN

## 1. Plateau-terminalized collar demand

Retain the canonical Target A notation. The original lattice population deficit is

`Delta_Lambda = N-|Y|`,

with

`Y=Y_0 sqcup Y_in`.

Write

`k=|Y_0|`,

and let the actual plateau-terminalized optimum have

`m^*=|X^*|`.

Define the residual collar demand after plateau terminalization by

`j_* := N-k-m^*`.

This is only a bookkeeping definition. Since

`|Y|=k+|Y_in|`,

we have the exact identity

`Delta_Lambda = j_* + m^* - |Y_in|`.                 (1.1)

Now split

`Y_in=Z sqcup O`.

Section 102 chooses a full `Z`-row basis `B` of size `|Z|`, with nonbasis set

`C=X^*\B=C^circ sqcup C^partial`.

The deep nonbasis set has

`|C^circ|=d_int`.

Therefore

`|C^partial| = m^* - |Z| - d_int`.                  (1.2)

## 2. Exact Schur-demand simplification

The canonical Schur demand is

`s = Delta_Lambda-d_int`.

Insert (1.1), `|Y_in|=|Z|+|O|`, and (1.2):

` s
 = j_* + m^* - |Z|-|O|-d_int
 = j_* + |C^partial|-|O|.`

### Theorem 1 — boundary count form of the Schur demand

`boxed{s = |C^partial|-|O|+j_*}`.                   (2.1)

Thus the remaining Target A demand contains no hidden bulk term. It is exactly:

1. boundary Schur-source count `|C^partial|`;
2. minus exterior sink count `|O|`;
3. plus the residual collar cardinality demand `j_*`.

This is the plateau-terminalized version of the older duality `h(G)>=j`.

Indeed Section 102 gives

`zeta=dim ker S=|C^partial|-rank S`,

and

`h(G)=|O|-rank S`.

Therefore

`zeta-s = h(G)-j_*`.                                 (2.2)

So the exact Schur endpoint

`zeta>=s`

is equivalent to the transparent right-deficiency endpoint

`h(G)>=j_*`.                                         (2.3)

## 3. Exact tube-shield target in sink-surplus form

Let `W=widehat W_Sigma` be the basis-closed tube shield of Section 174. For the components

`D_1,...,D_k`

of `K^*\|Sigma|`, put

`C_i=(C^partial\W) cap D_i`,

and let `O_i` be the surviving exterior sinks assigned to sector `D_i` by their surviving response neighbors.

A surviving `O` vertex may have no surviving response neighbor after shield deletion. Let

`O_iso={o in O\W: o has no surviving response neighbor}`.

Section 174.2 makes the nonisolated surviving `O` sets sector-unique, so the `O_i` are disjoint and partition

`(O\W)\O_iso`.

Define the signed sector surplus

`a_i=|C_i|-|O_i|`.

Then

`sum_i a_i
 = |C^partial|-|C^partial cap W|
   -|O|+|O cap W|+|O_iso|`.                          (3.1)

The positive-part population in Theorem 174.19 is

`P=sum_i max(0,a_i)`.

Write the total sink-heavy sector surplus as

`Q=sum_i max(0,-a_i)
  =sum_i max(0,|O_i|-|C_i|)`.

Since `P-Q=sum_i a_i`, the Section-174 sufficient condition

`P >= s+|W\C^partial|`                                (3.2)

can be rewritten exactly by substituting (2.1) and (3.1).

### Theorem 2 — exact three-sector sink-surplus target

Condition (3.2), hence the desired Schur nullity bound, is equivalent to

`boxed{
Q >= j_* + |W\O| - |O_iso|.
}`                                                       (3.3)

### Proof

Subtract (3.1) from the right side of (3.2):

`[s+|W\C^partial|]-sum_i a_i`

`=[|C^partial|-|O|+j_* + |W|-|C^partial cap W|]`

` -[|C^partial|-|C^partial cap W|-|O|+|O cap W|+|O_iso|]`

`=j_*+|W|-|O cap W|-|O_iso|`

`=j_*+|W\O|-|O_iso|`.

Since `P-sum_i a_i=Q`, (3.2) is equivalent to (3.3). `square`

## 4. Interpretation

The Section-174 theorem was previously phrased as the need for enough **positive left surplus**

`sum max(0,|C_i|-|O_i|)`.

The exact algebra shows the genuinely new information required from the stress-generated three-sector geometry is the dual statement:

> after paying the non-`O` shield vertices, the sectors must contain at least `j_*` units of **exterior-sink surplus** beyond their local Schur-source counts, up to the automatic credit from isolated surviving sinks.

The global signed terminal difference `|C^partial|-|O|` is already built into `s`; it is not the hard part. The hard gain is exactly the residual collar demand `j_*` plus shield overhead.

This sharpens the target for the source-labeled sector program. One no longer needs an opaque inequality involving `Delta_Lambda` or `d_int`; one needs to force sink-heavy sectors.

## 5. Interaction with the new source localization

The source-label crossing theorem and exterior-gate budget now imply that a zero-slack `X^*->A^0` matching is sector-preserving outside at most

`5|V(Sigma)|+12d+9`

vertices. The inner-source capacity theorem also gives

`|A^0 cap K^*| >= m^*-(12d+9)`.

These facts do not yet prove (3.3). Fixed-hull source capacity by itself does not force exterior-sink surplus; the two canonical source and response matchings remain independent.

The exact remaining bridge is therefore:

> use the nearly full genuine source inside `K^*`, the sector-preserving source labels, or the locked three-channel stress to prove the sink-surplus inequality (3.3), or construct a strict sub-`D` competitor.

## 6. Regression checks

- No identification is made between the separate `X^*->A^0` and `Y_in->A^0` saturating matchings.
- No common-neighbor SDR is assumed.
- Isolated surviving `O` vertices are retained explicitly as the favorable term `|O_iso|`.
- Shielded `O` vertices are not charged twice; their contribution is absorbed by `|W\O|`.
- The reformulation is exact algebra and does not use planarity to infer nullity.

